.TH remove-old-kernels "1" "31 Jan 2026" "remove-old-kernels-1.0.6" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
remove-old-kernels \- Utility to remove obsolete kernels in Mageia Systems using urpme.
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBremove-old-kernels\fP [\fI\-option\fP]

\fBrok\fP [\fI\-option\fP] 

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBremove-old-kernels\fR runs automatically under cron.weekly using
configuration settings in /etc/remove-old-kernels.cfg. This is the default in
order to avoid system failure due to old kernels filling the root file system.
.br
For most users it can be left, as installed, to do it's job without any user
intervention or concern.

.br
It keeps the default (3) kernels of EACH kernel flavour, NOT 3 in total. If
there are kernels of various flavours installed, then only the flavours with
more than 3 would see any removed.

.br
In a LIVE system with persistence only one kernel plus the original is kept,
since only those two are bootable.

.br
It will never remove the running kernel, or the original kernel in a LIVE system
with persistence.

.br
If the running kernel has been accidentally removed by some utility since boot
then it will report the error and abort, removing nothing.

\fBremove-old-kernels\fP also allows interactive command\-line removal of kernel
packages using various options.

\fBremove-old-kernels\fR with no arguments as a regular user, will display the
current kernel(s) installed, the one in use, marked with 'U', and the ones it
suggests to remove in red, if any.
.br
This is a good way to make a quick check without any risk of error.

\fBremove-old-kernels\fP is also available from the Mageia main menu system in
graphical desktops for ease of use by users unfamiliar with the command line.
Usually found under Tools -> System Tools. It will ask for the root password.

In automatic mode a \fBlog\fP is kept at \fB/var/log/remove-old-kernels.log\fP.
The log is limited to \fB1000\fP lines (up to a year) and will then start to
remove the oldest entries whilst adding new ones. See the \fB\-l\fP option to
view the log.
.br

.SH DISPLAY

.TP 
\fB\The header bar.
.br
1 - The system release information.
.br
2 - The number of kernels found in the /boot directory
.br
3 - Whether the automatic mode is on (1) or off (0). '0' is highlighted in red
as a warning when off.
.br
4 - The number of kernels to be kept per flavour.
.br
5 - Whether in advanced mode. e.g.| Q | Not shown when OFF.
.br
6 - The alternate configuration file in use. Not shown for the main
configuration file. e.g. F:1

.TP 
\fB\The list columns.
.br
1 - shows the number of kernels found of each flavour.
.br
2 - indicates whether the kernel will be kept or removed.
.br
3 - shows a key letter to indicate the reason for keeping. Run 'rok \-h' for key.
.br
4 - shows the full kernel package name concerned.
.br
5 - shows the date and time that the kernel was installed.

.SH CONFIGURATION

\fIThe configuration file\fP is \fB/etc/remove-old-kernels.cfg\fP which may be
edited manually if desired.
.br
There are several options below which can also be used more conveniently to
change settings in the configuration file
from the command line when \fIrun as root\fP.
.br
Several alternate configuration files may be added manually if desired. They
must be named as follows:
.br
\fB/etc/remove-old-kernels_N.cfg\fP where \fBN\fP is a digit from 1 to 9.
.br
A template configuration file is provided containing default values which may be
copied:
.br
\fB/usr/share/doc/remove-old-kernels/remove-old-kernels_N.cfg.template\fP
.br
To use a \fInew\fP alternate configuration file for the \fIcurrent\fP run you
can use the \fB\-f N\fP option, or \fB\-F N\fP to switch to it
\fIpersistently\fP.
.br
NOTE that any changes like \fIauto ON/OFF\fP, or \fIthe number of kernels to
keep\fP, that are made while running an alternate configuration
will be saved to THAT configuration file. They will not affect the default
'main' configuration.
.br
To switch back to the \fIoriginal\fP configuration use \fB\-F 0\fP.

.SH OPTIONS

.TP
\fB\-h\fP, \fB\-?\fP
help

Displays the available options and a key to column 3 of the display.

.TP
\fB\-n\fP \fIN\fP
Set the number of kernels to keep. ONLY for the current run. (No effect in LIVE
Systems)

This parameter sets the number of kernels to keep (this time) in interactive
mode. It must be 2 or more, it's value may be restricted by the setting in
etc/dnf/dnf.conf if dnf is installed, in which case there is an on-screen
warning. dnf sets a default value of 3.

.TP
\fB\-N\fP \fIN\fP
Like \fB\-n\fP \fIN\fP but stores the 'number of kernels to keep' persistently
in the config file. e.g. \fB\-N 4\fP (No effect in LIVE Systems)

It must be run as root to take effect.

.TP
\fB\-a\fP
automatic mode. Run once with no questions asked.

Similar to the weekly cron job and will use settings from
/etc/remove-old-kernels.cfg.
.br
It may be used with the -n N option if desired. NOTE: No output is sent to the
log when run manually.

.TP
\fB\-A\fP \fI[1|0]\fP
Automatic mode ON/OFF switch.

This switches the automatic weekly run ON (1) or OFF (0).
It directly edits /etc/remove-old-kernels.cfg (or an alternate config file if
it is currently in use), setting CRON=1 or CRON=0 so must be run as root to
have any effect.

.TP
\fB\-c\fP
Special option for system automatic mode. This option is NOT intended for manual
use.

Checks if CRON=1 is in /etc/remove-old-kernels.cfg before running, or exits.
The output is logged to /var/log/remove-old-kernels.log. It is run by
/etc/cron.weekly/remove-old-kernels.cron which also trims the log to 1000 lines
if it is greater. This equates to something over one year of use for basic
systems. See the -l option for viewing the log.

.TP
\fB\-f\fP \fIN\fP
Switch to an alternate configuration file. ONLY for the current run.

N is any number from 1 to 9. Alternate configuration files need to be prepared
in advance.

.TP
\fB\-F\fP \fIN\fP
Switch to an alternate configuration file. Persistently. (Must be root)

N is any number from 0 to 9. (0 returns to main configuration file). Alternate
configuration files need to be prepared in advance.

.TP
\fB\-t\fP
Test mode.

A test mode where no kernel is removed, to show what would be done. NOTE: If
combined with options that change configuration then when run as root those
options will still work.

.TP
\fB\-p\fP
Preview mode.

Dry run showing the urpme commands that would be used.

.TP
\fB\-q\fP
Advanced mode, run once.

An advanced mode (developed for Mageia QA testers).
.br
In addition to the regular mode, when ON this will also NOT remove:
.br
A kernel devel package of the same version as a corresponding installed kernel,
unless the kernel is going to be removed as well.
.br
A kernel potentially needed by an installed virtualbox kernel latest.
.br
A kernel potentially needed by an installed xtables-addons kernel latest.

.TP
\fB\-Q\fP \fIN\fP
Advanced mode, persistent. OFF by default.
When ON, "Q" is displayed near the right end of the header bar.

N = 1 for enable, 0 to disable. Default disabled.

.TP
\fB\-l\fP
Display the log file. (Only created by the weekly automatic runs)
.br
The last ~1000 lines of the log are retained. If no log has yet been created
then it will exit.

.TP
\fB\-v\fP
Show the version of the installed remove-old-kernels package.

.SH EXAMPLES

.TP
\fBrok\fP
.br
A shorthand link to 'remove-old-kernels'. Run as root to remove kernels
or change settings.

.TP
\fBremove-old-kernels -n4\fP
.br
Remove old kernels interactively, this time, keeping 4 kernels of each flavour.

.TP
\fBrok -l\fP
.br
Display the log file.

